          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Dramatic Works</strong><br>\nAfter his escape, Schiller's focus shifted to historical dramas, including \"Don Carlos\" and \"Mary Stuart.\" These works reflected his deep interest in the struggle for moral and political integrity and often highlighted individual resistance against oppressive structures.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Friendship with Goethe</strong><br>\nSchiller's friendship with Johann Wolfgang von Goethe began in 1794 and led to a productive period for both writers. They challenged each other's ideas, leading to a rich period of literary and philosophical development known as Weimar Classicism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Historical Studies and Professorship</strong><br>\nSchiller also delved into historical studies, accepting a history professorship at the University of Jena. His historical works contributed significantly to the development of historiography as a scholarly discipline.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
