            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Poetry and Aesthetics</strong><br>\nAlongside his plays and historical writings, Schiller wrote poetry, which included works such as the \"Ode to Joy,\" famously set to music by Ludwig van Beethoven. His aesthetic essays discuss the role of art and beauty, influencing future art and literary criticism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Illness and Productivity</strong><br>\nDespite battling illness for much of his life, Schiller remained remarkably productive. His later plays and poems are characterized by a vigorous style and continued to explore complex ethical and philosophical themes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Schiller's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nSchiller died in 1805 at the age of 45. His works, espousing ideals of freedom, justice, and beauty, made him a pivotal figure of German literature. His legacy is that of a poet who believed in the enlightening power of art for society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
